    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the household membership-level ceiling and made the test suite pass. Two changes, both in `OwnerRestControllerV1`:\n\n**1. The cap itself (the requested rule).** In `toOwnerDtoWithBulkWarning`, the owner's computed `membershipLevel` is now passed through a new `capMembershipLevel(owner, level)`:\n- It finds the maximum membership level among the *other* members of the owner's household (existing owners with the same `householdId`, excluding the owner itself and any soft-deleted owner).\n- The returned level is `min(level, maxHouseholdMemberLevel + 1)`.\n- When the owner has no `householdId` or no other household member, no cap applies and the level is returned unchanged \u2014 matching *\"With no existing household member no cap applies.\"*\n- Each member's level is its own base level (computed non-recursively), so the cap can't depend on other already-capped values.\n\n**2. Removing a contradictory legacy check (required to even reach the cap).** The new test creates two owners in the same household (same last name + postcode) with distinct telephones and expects both to be created. That was being rejected with a 409 by `requireNoHouseholdDuplicate`.\n\nI want to flag this clearly since it goes slightly beyond the literal rule statement: I removed `requireNoHouseholdDuplicate` (call + method). I did this because the codebase's own documentation says the consolidated `identityKey` check *\"replaces the former separate telephone, email and household checks,\"* and `requireUniqueIdentity` already handles true duplicates. `requireNoHouseholdDuplicate` was a leftover of that superseded design and directly contradicted both the new test's premise (\"same household, unique telephone \u2192 allowed\") and the existing `markPossibleDuplicate` logic (which flags \u2014 rather than rejects \u2014 same-household/different-telephone owners). Distinct-identity household members now coexist and are still flagged as possible duplicates; `sharesHousehold` still governs that flagging. No existing test covered the removed rejection.\n\nBoth the targeted test (`AcceptanceTest.coreCapsLevelByHousehold`) and the full `./mvnw test` build pass (BUILD SUCCESS).",
